222	    "to ensure the balance between exploitation and exploration in the system." + TRUST, "R2-trust")
223	
224	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R5  KL utility (Sec. 4.2, first paragraph + Eq. 5)
225	R5A = r"""% REVISED (Replacement 5): KL divergence defined; per-example, batch and client scores (tags keep the original numbering)
226	Each candidate client is denoted by $i \in \mathcal{C}$. At round $t$, the central server sends $w_{\text{global}}^t$ to all candidate clients. The Kullback--Leibler (KL) divergence of a distribution $P$ from a reference distribution $Q$ is $\mathrm{KL}(P \parallel Q)=\sum_{c}P(c)\log\frac{P(c)}{Q(c)}$; it is nonnegative, zero only when $P=Q$, and asymmetric (Kullback and Leibler 1951). For a classification task with label set $\mathcal{Y}$, client $i$ compares the class probabilities $P_i^t(\cdot\mid x)$ of its model after local training with the probabilities $Q^t(\cdot\mid x)$ of the unchanged global model received at round $t$, on the same client-local scoring examples (Amini et al. 2025). The per-example divergence is
227	\begin{equation}
228	d_i^t(x)=\sum_{c\in\mathcal{Y}}P_i^t(c\mid x)\log\frac{P_i^t(c\mid x)}{Q^t(c\mid x)}
229	\tag{5a}
230	\end{equation}
231	The scoring examples are divided into $B\ge 2$ nonempty batches $X_{i,b}^t$. The batch score and the client score, which weights every example equally, are
232	\begin{equation}
233	KL_{i,b}^t=\frac{1}{|X_{i,b}^t|}\sum_{x\in X_{i,b}^t}d_i^t(x)
234	\tag{5b}
235	\end{equation}
236	\begin{equation}
237	\Delta KL_i^t=\frac{\sum_{b=1}^{B}|X_{i,b}^t|\,KL_{i,b}^t}{\sum_{b=1}^{B}|X_{i,b}^t|}
238	\label{eq:kl}
239	\end{equation}
240	The client-level KL score $\Delta KL_i^t$ (the symbol $\Delta$ denotes local--global disagreement, not a change over rounds) measures post-training disagreement between local and global predictions. It is a heuristic indicator of locally learnable information, not a reduction in divergence and not evidence that aggregating the update improves the global model."""
241	block("Each participating client is denoted by", "\\label{eq:kl}\n\\end{equation}", R5A, "R5-kl")
242	
243	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R4  idle counters
244	R4 = r"""% REVISED (Replacement 4): idle counter increments for unselected clients; bound derived; no guarantee claimed
245	The idle counter $r_i^t$ is the number of consecutive committed rounds before round $t$ in which client $i$ was not selected. It is held by the server and initialised as $r_i^1=0$ for all $i\in\mathcal{C}$. After each committed round it is updated by Eq.~(7), where $C_k^t$ denotes the cohort selected at round $t$; rounds whose staged state is discarded leave the counters unchanged.
246	
247	\begin{equation}
248	r_i^{t+1} =
249	\begin{cases}
250	0 & \text{if } i \in C_k^t  \\
251	r_i^t + 1 & \text{if } i \notin C_k^t
252	\end{cases}
253	\label{eq:idle_update}
254	\end{equation}
255	Selection resets a counter to zero and every non-selected client increases its counter by one, so $r_i^t\ge 0$ grows linearly while a client waits. The fairness term of Eq.~(6), $F_i^t = 1 + r_i^t/(1+\overline{r^t})$ with the mean idle count $\overline{r^t}$ of Eq.~(19), is therefore bounded: since $r_i^t\le N\,\overline{r^t}$, it satisfies $1 \le F_i^t < N+1$. The term raises the priority of long-waiting clients, but it guarantees neither a maximum waiting time nor participation, because the mixing coefficient $a_i^t$ of Eq.~(8) can down-weight it; participation fairness is therefore evaluated empirically through the JFI of Eq.~(24)."""
256	block("The counter of idle rounds", "\\label{eq:idle_update}\n\\end{equation}", R4, "R4-idle")
257	
258	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R6  controller, priority
259	R6 = r"""% REVISED (Replacement 6): well-posed bounded feedback rule; priority renamed Pi; caveats stated
260	The proposed control mechanism is a bounded feedback rule that guides the system in determining whether to shift toward informativeness or fairness. It is not a Lyapunov function: no stability, convergence or fairness guarantee is established, and the term ``Lyapunov-inspired'' refers only to the feedback structure. The mixing coefficient $a_i^t \in [0,1]$, with $a_i^0=0.5$, is defined as follows:
261	% AUTHORS: confirm beta=0.9, lambda=1 and a^0=0.5 are the values to be reported (the original text never stated lambda)
262	\begin{equation}
263	a_i^t = \mathrm{clip}\left(
264	\beta \cdot a_i^{t-1} + (1-\beta)
265	\frac{
266	\nu^t \cdot (\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t)
267	}{
268	\lambda \cdot F_i^t + \left|\nu^t \cdot (\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t)\right|
269	},\ 0,\ 1\right)
270	\end{equation}
271	
272	Here $\mathrm{clip}(x,0,1)=\min\{1,\max\{0,x\}\}$, $\beta=0.9$ is an exponential moving average smoothing parameter and $\lambda=1$ is a fairness regularization coefficient. Since $F_i^t \ge 1$ and $\lambda>0$, the denominator is positive. The term $\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t$ provides a directional signal for decision-making, and the update smooths toward a signed feedback target in $[-1,1]$ before clipping to $[0,1]$. Because $F_i^t$ usually exceeds $\Delta KL_i^t$, a positive $\nu^t$ tends to lower $a_i^t$ and a negative $\nu^t$ tends to raise it.
273	
274	The fairness value increases when a client remains unselected, forcing other metrics in the priority score to encourage its selection as in Eq. (9), where the contribution of client $i$ during communication round $t$ is represented by the priority score $\Pi_i^t$. This score combines two main factors such as: fairness and utility while weighting them through a control mechanism by tuning the control parameter $a$, allowing the framework to prioritize fairness or utility depending on the global model's needs for either exploitation or exploration. The two terms of Eq. (9) are combined on their original scales; since $F_i^t \ge 1$ while $\Delta KL_i^t$ is typically much smaller than one, the fairness term can dominate the priority, and the utility term then acts mainly as a tie-breaker among clients with similar waiting times. Section 5 reports the values taken by $a_i^t$ and a variant in which both terms are rescaled to a common range.
275	\begin{equation}
276	\Pi_i^t = a_i^t \cdot \Delta KL_i^t + (1 - a_i^t)\, F_i^t
277	\label{eq:priority}
278	\end{equation}"""
279	block("The proposed control mechanism is inspired by the Lyapunov", "\\label{eq:priority}\n\\end{equation}", R6, "R6-controller")
280	
281	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R3  deviation signal and causal reference
282	R3 = r"""% REVISED (Replacement 3): causal reference (no baseline run, no gamma); signal direction follows Eq. (8)
283	The deviation control signal $\nu^t$ is computed on the server from completed rounds only. It is the difference between a reference convergence rate $\Omega^t$ and the relative validation-loss reduction $\rho^{t-1}$ of the previous round (Eq.~(15)), as shown in Eq.~(10), and it is the feedback input of Eq.~(8). A positive $\nu^t$ means that the previous round converged more slowly than the reference; a non-positive value means that it converged at least as fast.
284	\begin{equation}
285	\nu^t = \Omega^t - \rho^{t-1}\qquad (t \ge 2)
286	\label{eq:deviation}
287	\end{equation}
288	
289	\begin{equation}
290	\Omega^t = \beta_{\text{ref}}\,\Omega^{t-1} + (1-\beta_{\text{ref}})\,\rho^{t-2}\qquad (t \ge 3)
291	\label{eq:reference_rate}
292	\end{equation}
293	
294	Equation (11) defines the reference rate $\Omega^t$ as an exponential moving average, with smoothing constant $\beta_{\text{ref}}\in[0,1)$ ($\beta_{\text{ref}}=0.9$), of the loss reductions of earlier completed rounds of the same run. It is initialised with $\rho^{0}=0$, $\nu^{1}=0$ and $\Omega^{2}=0$, since the first round has no predecessor. The reference therefore requires no run of another algorithm, no acceleration index, no test data and no calibration traffic: it uses only the validation losses that the server already computes for Eq.~(15). Its effect on the mixing coefficient follows from Eq.~(8): since $\Delta KL_i^t - F_i^t$ is usually negative ($F_i^t\ge 1$), $\nu^t>0$ tends to lower $a_i^t$ and shifts the priority toward fairness, whereas $\nu^t\le 0$ tends to raise $a_i^t$ and shifts it toward utility. The signal thus measures how the latest round compares with the run's own recent progress, not with an external benchmark."""
295	block("The deviation control signal $\\nu^t$ is computed on the server side", "and $\\gamma$ denotes an acceleration index.", R3, "R3-reference")
296	
297	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R5  uncertainty penalty + stability/fitness
298	R5B = r"""% REVISED (Replacement 5/6): batch variance defined with its mean (12a); fitness uses the renamed priority
299	Client-side uncertainty is introduced into the fitness score as a variance based penalty term. It quantifies the dispersion of the KL score across several small batches of the local client's dataset. It measures differences between scoring batches; it does not by itself separate noisy data from legitimate heterogeneity or rare classes. The uncertainty penalty term for client \textit{i} is defined from the batch mean (12a) and the sample variance (12):
300	
301	\begin{equation}
302	\overline{KL}_i^t = \frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B} KL_{i,b}^t
303	\tag{12a}
304	\end{equation}
305	
306	\begin{equation}
307	\sigma_i^2 = \frac{1}{B-1} \sum_{b=1}^{B} \left( KL_{i,b}^t - \overline{KL}_i^t \right)^2
308	\label{eq:variance}
309	\end{equation}
310	
311	Where $B\ge 2$ indicates the total count of scoring batches (the sample variance is undefined for fewer batches), $\overline{KL}_i^t$ represents the average KL score over batches for client $i$, and $KL_{i,b}^t$ is the KL score of batch $b$ (Eq. (5b)).
312	
313	The stability score is derived from Eqs. (5) and (12). This score is used for weighting the local model updates in aggregation step. It prioritizes the informative and stable clients over less informative ones to stabilize and accelerate the convergence in heterogeneous data environments without affecting fairness. The formula of stability score is defined as follows:
314	
315	\begin{equation}
316	s_i^t = \Delta KL_i^t - \mu \sigma_i^2
317	\label{eq:stability}
318	\end{equation}
319	The raw score may be negative; it is converted to nonnegative aggregation weights in Eq. (18). The following equation summarizes the calculation formula of the composite fitness score:
320	\begin{equation}
321	Fitness_i^t = \Pi_i^t - \mu \sigma_i^2
322	\label{eq:fitness}
323	\end{equation}"""
324	block("Client-side uncertainty is introduced into the fitness score", "\\label{eq:fitness}\n\\end{equation}", R5B, "R5-variance-fitness")
325	
326	# ------------------------------------------------------------------ R7 cohort + R3 convergence efficiency

