\subsection{Analysis of the selection score}\label{sec:score_analysis}
This subsection examines the components of the selection score of Eqs.~(5)--(14). It uses a ResNet-18 with frozen early layers, 100 clients, 100 rounds, cohorts of 5 to 10 clients, 3 seeds for the ablations (5 for the utility analysis) and 95\% bootstrap intervals; the absolute accuracies therefore differ from those of Tables~1 and~2, and all variants in Table~\ref{tab:mixing} differ from FedFACS only in the mixing coefficient $a$.

\textbf{Utility of the KL score.} For every candidate client, in rounds 1, 5, 10, 25, 50 and 100, we computed the marginal gain, i.e. the reduction in global validation loss obtained when that client's update alone is added to the global model with weight $1/m_t$, and its Spearman rank correlation $\rho$ with the client indicators of Table~\ref{tab:kl_validity}. The KL score is positively associated with the gain under every label-skew partition ($\rho=0.39$ to $0.73$). The global cross-entropy of the client's examples is the stronger predictor under label skew but reverses its sign under label noise ($\rho=-0.77$), because clients with corrupted labels have a high loss and degrade the aggregate; the KL score shows no such reversal ($\rho=0.12$, not significant). Selecting with a loss-based utility instead of KL changes accuracy by $+0.57$ [$-0.33$, $+1.64$] points on CIFAR-10 and $+0.25$ [$-0.08$, $+0.48$] on FMNIST, i.e. not detectably. We therefore regard the KL score as a valid, label-free and noise-robust indicator, not as the strongest predictor under label skew. Compared with the raw parameter drift $\lVert w_i^t-w_{\mathrm{global}}^t\rVert$ (Table~\ref{tab:kl_drift}), the KL score is the better predictor under the two-class partition on both datasets and is not distinguishable from it under Dirichlet $\alpha=0.1$.

