\label{tab:kl_drift}
\begin{tabular*}{\journalfloatwidth}{@{\extracolsep{\fill}}llccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Data partition & KL score & Drift & KL $-$ drift \\
\midrule
CIFAR-10 & Dirichlet $\alpha=0.1$ & 0.41 & 0.24 & 0.17 [$-$0.17, 0.43] \\
         & Two classes per client & 0.71 & 0.33 & \textbf{0.37} [0.32, 0.43] \\
FMNIST   & Dirichlet $\alpha=0.1$ & 0.56 & 0.64 & $-$0.08 [$-$0.34, 0.11] \\
         & Two classes per client & 0.75 & 0.50 & \textbf{0.25} [0.09, 0.46] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\end{table}

\textbf{Fairness term and mixing coefficient.} Table~\ref{tab:mixing} compares the feedback rule of Eq.~(8) with constant coefficients on Dirichlet $\alpha=0.5$. Without the fairness term ($a=1$) the Jain index falls to 0.57 and 0.35, 10.0\% and 18.7\% of the clients are never selected and accuracy decreases by 2.20 and 2.02 points, so the fairness term is indispensable. The feedback rule improves on the balanced setting $a=0.5$ by 1.62 [1.13, 2.33] points on CIFAR-10 and 1.25 [1.13, 1.41] on FMNIST, with a Jain index of 0.996--0.997 against 0.934 and a longest waiting time of 11 rounds against 15--16. The coefficient settles at a median of 0.04 (mean 0.10; 5th--95th percentile 0.005--0.32), so selection is led by the fairness term and the utility term mainly orders clients with similar waiting times. Small constant coefficients reach the same accuracy and fairness as the feedback rule (difference for $a=0.05$: $+0.20$ [$-0.25$, $+0.89$] on CIFAR-10 and $+0.13$ [$+0.04$, $+0.31$] on FMNIST; for $a=0.10$: $-0.23$ [$-1.43$, $+0.99$] and $-0.46$ [$-0.65$, $-0.20$]). The feedback rule therefore attains near-uniform participation (JFI $\ge 0.996$, no unselected client) at accuracy parity with the best constant without requiring that constant to be chosen in advance; an advantage over a well-tuned constant is not established with three seeds. Rescaling the utility and its variance to the scale of the fairness term does not change accuracy ($+0.20$ [$-0.51$, $+1.14$] and $-0.04$ [$-0.44$, $+0.24$]).

