         & Constant $a=0.05$       & \textbf{71.51} $\pm$ 0.32 & \textbf{0.998} & 0 & \textbf{10.0} & 61.2 $\pm$ 3.8 \\
         & Constant $a=0.10$       & 71.08 $\pm$ 0.36 & 0.997 & 0 & 10.7 & 59.1 $\pm$ 6.7 \\
         & Constant $a=0.5$        & 69.69 $\pm$ 0.46 & 0.934 & 0 & 16.0 & 43.3 $\pm$ 3.3 \\
         & Constant $a=1$ (no fairness) & 69.11 $\pm$ 0.53 & 0.566 & 10.0 & 100 & 50.9 $\pm$ 7.6 \\
\midrule
FMNIST   & FedFACS (feedback rule) & 85.07 $\pm$ 0.37 & 0.997 & 0 & 11.0 & \textbf{74.9} $\pm$ 1.1 \\
         & Constant $a=0.05$       & \textbf{85.20} $\pm$ 0.36 & \textbf{0.998} & 0 & \textbf{10.3} & 72.9 $\pm$ 2.7 \\
         & Constant $a=0.10$       & 84.61 $\pm$ 0.43 & 0.997 & 0 & 10.7 & 74.6 $\pm$ 6.0 \\
         & Constant $a=0.5$        & 83.82 $\pm$ 0.26 & 0.934 & 0 & 15.3 & 71.8 $\pm$ 6.9 \\
         & Constant $a=1$ (no fairness) & 83.05 $\pm$ 0.75 & 0.353 & 18.7 & 100 & 68.3 $\pm$ 5.8 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular*}
\end{table}

\textbf{Uncertainty penalty and sensitivity.} The batch variance does not separate noisy from clean clients (rank AUC 0.45 [0.39, 0.52]), so it acts as a stabilising penalty and not as a noise detector; removing it lowers accuracy by 0.54 [$-0.80$, $-0.40$] points on CIFAR-10 and 0.68 [$-0.93$, $-0.50$] on FMNIST. On FMNIST the accuracy is stable for $B\in\{4,8\}$ and $\mu\in\{1,10\}$ (85.07, 85.19, 85.02), for $\lambda=10$ (85.07) and for $\beta=0.5$ (85.08), and is lower for $B=2$ (84.30) and $\mu=0$ (84.39). When $\nu^t>0$ the mean coefficient decreased in 96.7\% of 1,827 rounds, as Eq.~(8) predicts.

