\textbf{Uncertainty penalty and sensitivity.} The batch variance does not separate noisy from clean clients (rank AUC 0.45 [0.39, 0.52]), so it acts as a stabilising penalty and not as a noise detector; removing it lowers accuracy by 0.54 [$-0.80$, $-0.40$] points on CIFAR-10 and 0.68 [$-0.93$, $-0.50$] on FMNIST. On FMNIST the accuracy is stable for $B\in\{4,8\}$ and $\mu\in\{1,10\}$ (85.07, 85.19, 85.02), for $\lambda=10$ (85.07) and for $\beta=0.5$ (85.08), and is lower for $B=2$ (84.30) and $\mu=0$ (84.39). When $\nu^t>0$ the mean coefficient decreased in 96.7\% of 1,827 rounds, as Eq.~(8) predicts.

