426	% REVISED (Replacement 5/6): batch variance defined with its mean (12a); fitness uses the renamed priority
427	Client-side uncertainty is introduced into the fitness score as a variance based penalty term. It quantifies the dispersion of the KL score across several small batches of the local client's dataset. It measures differences between scoring batches; it does not by itself separate noisy data from legitimate heterogeneity or rare classes. The uncertainty penalty term for client \textit{i} is defined from the batch mean (12a) and the sample variance (12):
428	
429	\begin{equation}
430	\overline{KL}_i^t = \frac{1}{B}\sum_{b=1}^{B} KL_{i,b}^t
431	\tag{12a}
432	\end{equation}
433	
434	\begin{equation}
435	\sigma_i^2 = \frac{1}{B-1} \sum_{b=1}^{B} \left( KL_{i,b}^t - \overline{KL}_i^t \right)^2
436	\label{eq:variance}
437	\end{equation}
438	
439	Where $B\ge 2$ indicates the total count of scoring batches (the sample variance is undefined for fewer batches), $\overline{KL}_i^t$ represents the average KL score over batches for client $i$, and $KL_{i,b}^t$ is the KL score of batch $b$ (Eq. (5b)).
440	
441	The stability score is derived from Eqs. (5) and (12). This score is used for weighting the local model updates in aggregation step. It prioritizes the informative and stable clients over less informative ones to stabilize and accelerate the convergence in heterogeneous data environments without affecting fairness. The formula of stability score is defined as follows:
442	
443	\begin{equation}
444	s_i^t = \Delta KL_i^t - \mu \sigma_i^2
445	\label{eq:stability}
446	\end{equation}
447	The raw score may be negative; it is converted to nonnegative aggregation weights in Eq. (18). The following equation summarizes the calculation formula of the composite fitness score:
448	\begin{equation}
449	Fitness_i^t = \Pi_i^t - \mu \sigma_i^2
450	\label{eq:fitness}
451	\end{equation}
452	
453	Where $\mu \ge 0$ is a regularization coefficient. Finally, each client sends its fitness score ${Fitness}_i^t$ to the server for evaluation; the server holds the number of consecutive idle rounds $r_i^t$. When the client is selected $i\in C_k^t$, it uploads its local model updates $w_i^t$, its stability score $s_i^t$ and its sample count $n_i$ for aggregation, and the server resets its idle counter at commit.
454	
455	On the server-side, it receives fitness scores from all clients for evaluation. The server ranks the clients according to their fitness scores and adaptively determines the cohort size $m_t=|C_k^t|$ according to the convergence efficiency observed in the previous communication round, namely the relative reduction $\rho^{t-1}$ of the validation loss as shown in Eq. (15). Two predefined integer bounds, $C_{k_{\min}}$ and $C_{k_{\max}}$ (fixed in advance for each configuration), regulate the allowable cohort size range (McMahan et al. 2017; Li et al. 2020), thereby enabling an adaptive number of clients to be selected for aggregation while maintaining communication efficiency and mitigating network congestion, as defined in Eq. (16). Here $L_{global}^{t}$ is the validation loss of the global model after round $t$ and $L_{global}^{0}$ that of the initial model; a negative $\rho^{t}$ records a loss increase and is retained, while a zero previous loss or a nonfinite value invalidates the round.
456	% REVISED (Replacement 3): Eq. (15) written for the completed round t (selection in round t uses rho^{t-1})
457	\begin{equation}
458	\rho^{t} = \frac{L_{{global}}^{t-1} - L_{{global}}^{t}}{L_{{global}}^{t-1}},\qquad L_{{global}}^{t-1}>0
459	\label{eq:convergence}
460	\end{equation}
461	
462	% REVISED (Replacement 7): integer cohort size within its bounds
463	\begin{equation}
464	m_t = \min\left\{C_{k_{\max}},\ \max\left\{C_{k_{\min}},\ \left\lfloor C_{k_{\min}} + (C_{k_{\max}} - C_{k_{\min}})\big[1-\mathrm{clip}(\rho^{t-1},0,1)\big] + \tfrac{1}{2}\right\rfloor\right\}\right\}
465	\label{eq:ck_selection}
466	\end{equation}
467	
468	When $\rho^{t-1}$ indicates sharp improvement, the cohort size is dynamically decreased reducing communication overhead. In contrast, during stagnation and degradation (including a loss increase), the cohort size increases to $C_{k_{\max}}$, requesting updates from more informative clients to improve global model accuracy and push it out of a local optimum; initialisation ($\rho^0=0$) also selects $C_{k_{\max}}$. The rule is a heuristic interpolation and is not shown to give a minimal or optimal cohort.
469	
470	% REVISED (Replacement 8/9): nonnegative normalised weights; staged commit; Eq. (19) with N
471	Once the selected clients submit their local updates, the stability scores are converted into aggregation weights. For the selected clients let $s_{i,+}^t=\max(s_i^t,0)$ be the positive part of the raw score and $H^t=\sum_{j\in C_k^t}s_{j,+}^t$ its cohort sum. Following this, the server carries out a stability-weighted aggregation, which is formulated as follows (Eq. (17) restates Eq. (4)):
472	
473	\begin{equation}
474	w_{{global}}^{t+1} = \sum_{i \in C_k^t} S_i^t \, w_i^t
475	\label{eq:stability_weighted_update}
476	\end{equation}
477	
478	\begin{equation}
479	S_i^t =
480	\begin{cases}
481	\dfrac{s_{i,+}^t}{H^t}, & H^t>0,\\[1.5ex]
482	\dfrac{n_i}{\sum_{j\in C_k^t} n_j}, & H^t=0
483	\end{cases}
484	\label{eq:stability_norm}
485	\end{equation}
486	
487	For numerical stability the first branch is evaluated as $z_i^t/\sum_{j\in C_k^t}z_j^t$ with $z_i^t=s_{i,+}^t/\hat{s}^{\,t}$ and $\hat{s}^{\,t}=\max_{j\in C_k^t}s_{j,+}^t$. Both branches give $S_i^t\ge 0$ and $\sum_{i\in C_k^t}S_i^t=1$, and they are defined for negative scores and for identical client models (all scores zero). Frozen tensors and nontrainable buffers keep their round-start values. A selected client with $S_i^t=0$ incurs upload cost and resets its idle counter but does not contribute to that round's aggregate.
488	
489	After aggregation, the server evaluates the candidate model on a separate validation set and stages $\rho^t$, $\Omega^{t+1}$, $\nu^{t+1}$, $r_i^{t+1}$ and $\overline{r^{t+1}}$ (Eq.~(19)). The model, the feedback state and the counters are committed together only if all numerical and protocol checks pass; otherwise the staged state is discarded and the consumed resources are still recorded. Messages carry run and round identifiers, and duplicate or stale messages are rejected. The committed global model parameters $w_{{global}}^{t+1}$, $\overline{r^{t+1}}$, and $\nu^{t+1}$ are then broadcast to all $N$ candidate clients for the following training round.
490	
491	\begin{equation}
492	\overline{r^{t+1}} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^{N} r_i^{t+1}
493	\label{eq:avg_idle_correct}
494	\end{equation}
